Jacek Dziedzic wrote:

>    Hi there!

Hi!

>    I'm quite new to nethack and I'm quite happy with my recent
> wizard character, but I'd like some hints to help me on!

Let me first point at the fact that once every two weeks, a spoiler is
posted in this newsgroup with tips and tricks for new players. This
spoiler will answer lots of newbie-questions, without giving away too
much. After all, it's more fun to find things out for yourself, isn't
it?

>    My usual trouble #1 with wizards was that they got hungry
> until they died -- now guess what -- my last wizard was
> generated with a ring of slow digestion -- now that's something
> to be happy about! What's more, this ring is cursed (by a
> kobold shaman, I believe) -- does it only mean I can't take
> it, or does it differ in some other manner from a normal
> ring of slow digestion? It seems to work fine -- I'm satiated
> all the time -- very nice for my wizard. :)

Three points:

1. Finding a ring of slow digestion or starting with one is a good
thing, but in no way critical. There are many ways of solving the hunger
problem. You can eat normal food items, of course. You may also eat the
corpses of monsters you slaughtered, although some of these may be
poisonous (it will take some trial and error (death) to find out which
are safe to eat and which are not). Lastly, once you get very hungry or
starving, you may in a last attempt use the #pray command, which, if you
don't overuse it, will provide you with a full tummy.

2. A cursed ring merely cannot be taken off, which, in the case of slow
digestion only prohibits you from wearing two other rings. Note that
wearing any ring consumes a bit of food, so other rings than slow
digestion have a little negative effect on you as well as the positive
effect they might be giving. Don't ever wear a ring when you don't know
beforehand whether it's cursed or not. Note that a very important use of
an altar is that when you drop an item on it, the game will tell you if
it's cursed, blessed or uncursed. In this newsgroup, this is called
determining the BUC-status.

3. Being satiated isn't nice. It prevents you from eating things that
you really wish to eat *now*, since, when you eat while satiated, you
always have a chance to die immediately. If you take care not to be
satiated at any time, you always can eat whatever you wish to eat. This
is especially important if you have just killed something that left a
corpse which you want to eat.
Why would you eat corpses? Well, a lot of them convey so-called
"intrinsics" on you. For example, eating a white dragon gives you cold
resistance, while eating a red dragon corpse conveys fire resistance
(I'm deliberately giving examples that you won't encounter yet).
Note that corpses may only be safely eaten while they are still fresh,
or you might die because of rotten food poisoning. That's why you want
to be able to eat them *immediately*.

>    Now for the state I'm in -- I managed to visit the dungeon
> up to the 9 level or so in both branches. I found sokoban but
> did not dare it yet. I explored the mines for a few levels,
> including the level where there are a lot of guards and a few
> shops (is that what you guys call 'minetown'?). I found almost
> nothing of value in the shops. I found an altar of my alignment
> on... dlvl1 and when I came back there after my exploration
> and offered only one corpse, I got awarded Magicbane. That's
> my first artifact, so I'm quite excited about it.

Sokoban isn't very dangerous, and has lots of rewards. For example, food
is plentiful there, and every level has a ring and a wand. The last
(top) level has a bag of holding or an amulet of reflection in addition
to that. Don't try to "cheat" at the game, though: there are
consequences for that which you won't notice directly, but *do*
influence your game.

The mines are more dangerous, although the shops sometimes contain
valuable items. Also, Minetown always has an altar, which you may use to
BUC-identify items. Knowing whether items are or aren't cursed is
incredibly valuable, and you can use altars even if they're not of your
own god.

Obviously, the altar was co-aligned (devoted to your own god), or
offering there would have been costly to you (the priest there would
have attacked you). Getting Magicbane was very lucky for you: my wizards
use it all the time. It's usual for wizards, though, to be the first
gift from their god, so it's not very unusual.

>    Now for my major problems with the wizard. First of all --
> I haven't had a chance to identify *any* of my items. I found
> a few potions, a few scrolls, two wands and three rings
> and I have no idea what each one represents. How do I go
> about identifying them? In desperation I read all the
> scrolls I had, hoping to both identify them for later game
> and to find a scroll of identify, but that didn't do much.
> I do not know the spell of identify. So how do I identify
> my rings/potions/scrolls/wands quickly?

Reading scrolls at random is *very* dangerous, especially if you're not
sure whether or not they are cursed. Don't do it.

You can identify a few rings by wearing them, or by dropping them down a
sink (you will lose them that way, so only do this when you have more
than one of the same type). You will get a cryptic message when you try
this: the ring won't be actually identified. Of course, don't try them
on unless you know them not to be cursed.

Wands are easier: write something on the floor with your fingers, then
overwrite this with something else using a wand. The cryptic message you
may then get is clearer than the one a ring will give when dropped down
a sink, and will often identify the wand. Other than that, you may try
zapping the wand at an item, door, chest or monster and hope something
recognisable happens.

Potions are usually safe to quaff, especially when blessed. Don't try
them when they are cursed. #Dip something in a potion before you quaff
it: it might be a potion of polymorph.

About the scroll of identify: it is the cheapest scroll around. If you
have a few scrolls, take them to a shop that will buy scrolls and drop
them there. When asked if you want to sell them, don't. Just notice the
price you're offered. For a scroll of identify, you will never be
offered more than approximately 13 zorkmids, usually less.

>    Second of all -- I have St:12 and am constantly on the
> verge of being Burdened. Having a few potions, scrolls,
> books, wands, rings and daggers is the most I can afford.
> I'm carrying all the wands/rings/scrolls/potions because
> they are not identified. Is that the right thing to do?
> Do I need to carry the books? I realize that I will forget
> the spells I know with time, but should I perhaps lay these
> books in some safe location? What about other potentially
> useful items (I found a leash, a whistle, a towel, a blindfold,
> a tin opener, etc.) -- am I supposed to carry it all with me?
> If not, how do I store them safely in some place?
> Perhaps I should be stronger than St:12? I was St:11 initally,
> but pushing the boulders made me stronger.

You carry too much. For example, you should drop your spellbooks (and
remember where you left them). 20000 turns after learning a spell, you
will forget it, and that's when you need your books again to re-learn
that spell.

It's nice to have an unlocked box or chest somewhere. You can use this
chest to stash away all items you don't immediately need, but don't want
to throw away (after all, other creatures may use them against you if
you just leave them around). Monsters will never open a box or take
something out of them. Some items cannot be used by any monster
(spellbooks, for example), so you may leave those just anywhere (don't
forget where you left them, though).

A blindfold is one of the most useful items in the game, provided you've
eaten the corpse of a certain monster. Closing your eyes actually makes
you see things, in a way.

As for weapons, you only need Magicbane for hand-to-hand fighting, and a
few daggers for throwing. Drop the rest. You shouldn't be using weapons
in the first place. Your weapon is Force Bolt, until you get Magic
Missile.

>    Third of all -- how do I acquire lower AC as a wizard?
> I realize that most armour makes no sense for a wizard because
> it reduces spellcasting abilities. So... what kind of armour
> is 'safe' for a wizard? The only I've got besides my cloak
> of magic resistance is a cloak of displacement, or something
> like it, that hardly improves my AC, but confuses my opponents
> in a way. I had my AC at a measly 8 but unfortunately I put
> on a cursed helmet of <something bad> and now have AC 10. Duh.
> I realize this sucks -- how do I go about fixing it?

Any non-metallic armour is good for a wizard. Other armour impedes
spellcasting (with the exception of a Helmet of Brilliance). Once you
know armour not to be cursed, try it on. Its enchantment will
immediately be shown, and you can use the + command to see whether your
chances of succesfully casting spells have decreased in a dramatic way.

Your cloak of magic resistance is very useful, especially later in the
game. For now, I would choose the cloak of displacement instead: it
makes you *much* harder to hit, especially if you change your position
once in a while while you're fighting. And note that wielding Magicbane
*also* provides magic resistance, so you've already got the intrinsic.

The only risk when trying on uncursed or blessed armour are helmets:
some of these will auto-curse when you put them on, so helmets are
*never* safe to put on.

>    Fourth, and last, of all -- is this Magicbane I got better
> that my blessed +3 quarterstaff? I know I can find a few
> tables about it in the spoilers, but in real life -- will it
> make sense to use this Magicbane instead of my quarterstaff?
> If so, why? If not, why? Do my abilities to use the Magicbane
> rely on my dagger skill? If so, than good, because I've been
> practicing dagger! Can I have a shield in my other hand with
> the Magicbane or with the quarterstaff? Does it make sense
> for a wizard?

It's *much* better, especially if enchanted only a little bit (the
communis opinio here is that +2 is the best possible enchantment for
Magicbane). Next to doing damage, it will also inflict other things on
your enemies when you hit them (confuse them, probe them, ...), but the
higher it is enchanted, the less often these special abilities will show
up. Magicbane will also protect you from curses in some way. Finally,
any dagger has a +1 to hit, which will also come in useful.

Magicbane indeed uses the dagger skill, but don't bother using a shield.
It's metallic armour *and* impedes your freedom of movement, which will
both make spellcasting more difficult.

>    Also, a few of my characters had died on the oracle
> level after entering a room full of various animals.
> This time there was no such room on the oracle level
> -- how is it?

A room full of monsters may appear on any level, from a certain dungeon
depth onward (we call it a zoo). It's filled with random monsters, but
the Oracle level tends to be biased toward normal animals, which
reflects in its zoos as well.

>    OK, perhaps one more -- how do I stop my beloved tame large
> cat from attacking peaceful characters, other than locking
> it up or keeping it on a leash. Do characters like "peaceful
> hill orc" or alike help you in any way or do they only
> grunt on being #chatted to and ask for potions of full
> healing? I.e, should I really prevent my pets from attacking
> them, or let them kill them, pretending I don't notice and
> pick the goodies?

You can't stop your pet and, generally, you don't want to stop it. The
game in no way holds your pet's behaviour against you. Whatever it does,
only your pet will be held accountable for it (you may use this feature
in other contexts as well). I, myself, consider peaceful creatures an
annoyance, and let my pets kill those if in any way possible.

Now, there are a few creatures that you don't want your pet to attack.
These include, but are not limited to, shopkeepers, priests, and the
Oracle. The reason why is obvious: these creatures *are* useful to you.
For example, you may donate money to priests, sell things to or buy
things from shopkeepers to identify them by price, and buy oracularities
from the Pythia.

Another reason why you don't want your pet to attack peaceful creatures
is the same reason why you don't want them to attack hostile creatures:
the attacked creature might be more powerful than your pet, and kill it.
The Mine Town Captain of the Guard is an example of such peaceful
creatures.

